        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "I'm thinking through the design: a whimsical capybara with an exaggerated blocky head, tiny ears, round barrel body, and short chubby legs, with a small bird perched on its head for charm.\n\nFor extra story details, I'm considering adding a daisy flower tucked behind the ear and a tiny ladybug on the nose alongside the bird, keeping the overall look cute but not overloaded.\n\nNow I'm planning the skeleton structure—a spine chain running hips to chest to neck to head, a tail stub, four legs with correct toe counts for a capybara, jaw and ear joints, plus a separate bird skeleton (body, head, beak, wings, tail) parented under the capybara's head joint, all oriented with the capybara facing forward.\n\nI'm working out proportions: roughly 1.1-1.2 meters nose to rump, about 0.55-0.58 meters tall at the back, with a blunt boxy head and muzzle positioned at the front. I'm laying out the spine points along the z-axis from rump to neck base and sweeping an elliptical body tube along that path, but realizing my initial radius makes the back too tall and needs scaling down to match a realistic capybara proportion.\n\nI'm settling on a lower body axis with slightly smaller radii so the belly sits close to the ground, and planning short two-segment legs from shoulder to ankle with a slight bend, finishing each foot with a toe pad and small clawed toes.\n\nNow I'm working out the hind legs, placing the hip further back near the rump mass and considering a three-segment limb (thigh, shin, hock) with forward-then-back bends to get that characteristic capybara hind-leg posture.\n\nFor digits, I'm planning each toe as its own short one-span chain attached to the foot, tagged with a \"digit\" role -- four toes up front, three in back -- so each toe gets its own named joint for animation.\n\nThat gives roughly 28 toe joints total, which is comfortably under the 160-joint budget alongside spine, neck, head, jaw, ears, tail, and legs. Now I'm sketching the spine as a catmull chain running from pelvis through hips to the chest and neck base, keeping the hierarchy simple.\n\nI'm skipping a visible tail since capybaras barely have one, maybe just a tiny nub. For the body structure, I'll sweep a single path from rump through belly and chest into the neck, using varying radius functions so the rump is round, the belly fat, and the chest narrows as it flows into a separate neck joint leading up to the head.\n\nThinking through the proportions, a capybara's back is arched and highest near the rump, so I'll place the rump slightly higher than the chest. I'm laying out key points along the spine from the tail root through the hips, midbody, chest, and shoulder/neck base, each with its own radius to shape the barrel-like torso before it tapers up into the short neck and head.\n\nNow for the head: a large boxy muzzle with blunt nose, nostrils and eyes high up. I'll build it as a loft from the back of the skull to the nose tip, tapering the width/height through a few stations, using rounded caps and a circular cross-section to keep it soft rather than angular, plus a small dark nose pad at the tip.\n\nFor the jaw, I'll add a separate lower piece—a small wedge sweep beneath the muzzle to suggest the joint—along with an inked lip line and a couple of whimsical ivory/orange incisors peeking over the lower lip for character. Then I'm moving on to the eyes, picturing them big, glossy, and black for a playful look.\n\nFor the eyes themselves, I'm planning dark spheres with small white highlight dots placed high on the side, plus half-closed sleepy eyelids made from partial skin-toned hemispheres draped over the top half of each eye to capture that chill capybara expression.\n\nFor the ears, I'm keeping them small and rounded with dark inner coloring, sitting toward the top-back of the head, and placing a tiny bird perched between them on top of the skull. The overall coat will be a coarse reddish-brown fur to match the whimsical capybara aesthetic.\n\nFor color, I'm going with warm caramel brown for the main coat, lighter tan for the belly and muzzle, and darker tones for the nose and feet, with countershading and mottled strokes to give the fur texture and variety. Rather than a toon-cel style, I'll keep it mostly flat-painted with soft mottle plus scattered small fur-tuft cards on the rump, cheeks, and chin, along with whiskers, to achieve a cheerful storybook look.\n\nWriting paint color functions...\n\nFor a whimsical touch, I'm adding a small bluebird companion—blue back, rust breast, white belly—built from merged spheres with a tiny orange cone beak, calculating texel density for the overall paint coverage to keep detail crisp.\n\nI'll skip adding grass tufts or a base around the feet to keep the loose parts manageable, and I'll give the capybara a few thin tapered whiskers per side plus sparse chin hair for texture.\n\nFor the nose, I'm shaping a dark brown nose pad at the muzzle tip with two small dark nostril ellipsoids ringed lighter, matching the blunt, high-set nostril placement typical of capybaras. For the muzzle's boxy snout profile, I want to check how loft handles section shapes before building it, so I'm going to look at the builder source to see what options the loft function supports.\n\n",
